## How it was tested

- `api:registry`: new `ObjAddTimersTest` (unit): a fresh tradeable drop is sent as 100/300/self; an untradeable one as 0/300/self/never public; a re-send 51 ticks later as 49/249; the reveal as 0/200/other; a re-send after the reveal as other with the remaining time; a server obj as all 0; the obj on a private zone prot keeps `ownerId` and its timers.
- `api:registry`: new `ObjRegistrySplitOwnerTest` (unit, loads the cache): three bronze axes dropped as one obj with receiver A and owner B split into three objs that each keep receiver A, owner B and the timers.
- `api:player`: new `PlayerDropTimersTest` (unit): GE-tradeable 300/100; not GE-tradeable 300/never (even with `tradeable=true`); every clue and challenge scroll tier and the Mimic 6100/never; "Clue scroll", "Clue scroll (special)" and "Scroll box (easy)" keep the untradeable timers.
- In game, desktop RuneLite against this branch (recorded through RSProx): dropped bronze axe and noted bronze axes were sent with `reveal=100, despawn=300, ownership=self` and deleted 299 ticks after the drop tick, as in the official recordings; a dropped Clue scroll (easy) was sent with `despawn=6100, neverturnpublic=true` and stayed. The first run sent the trowel as tradeable, which is how the `tradeable` flag problem showed up; the fix is covered by the unit test.

## Checklist

- [x] Builds and the server boots
- [x] Tests only cover complex logic and anything that moves items (no dupes): the drop timers and the packet fields
- [x] Gamevals symbols instead of raw ids; game data in the cache, not Kotlin maps: tradeability from the cache; clue items by their cache names (no param or category covers exactly the clue, challenge scrolls and Mimic, see notes)
- [x] State in varbits/varps, not attrs: no new state besides the obj's own timers
